WN.lb(16,{"mt_xHF1RUrETs":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"After 2 h a candle is 18 cm. After 5 h it is 12 cm. Zoe says it loses 9 cm per hour, because 18 ÷ 2 = 9. What went wrong?","choices":["She divided one reading by its time; use change ÷ change: 6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm per hour","Nothing, 9 cm per hour is right","She should have done 12 ÷ 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"How much height was lost between the two readings, and over how long?","explain":"18 ÷ 2 assumes the candle started at 0 cm. Between the readings it lost 6 cm in 3 h, so the rate is 2 cm per hour."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A spring is 19 cm long with 2 kg hanging on it, and 28 cm long with 5 kg. It stretches steadily. How long is it with nothing hanging on it, in cm?","answer":13,"hint":"Stretch per kg = (28 − 19) ÷ (5 − 2). Then take 2 kg worth off 19 cm.","explain":"Rate = 9 ÷ 3 = 3 cm per kg. Start = 19 − 2 × 3 = 13 cm. So L = 13 + 3m."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"On one day the air is 9 °C at 1 km up and −4 °C at 3 km up, falling steadily. What is the rate of change?","choices":["−6.5 °C per km","6.5 °C per km","−13 °C per km"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change in temperature ÷ change in height. Is the temperature going up or down?","explain":"(−4 − 9) ÷ (3 − 1) = −13 ÷ 2 = −6.5 °C per km. It is negative because it gets colder higher up."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A tank drains so that V = {=r*n} − {r}t litres after t minutes. After how many minutes is it empty?","answer":"n","hint":"Empty means V = 0. Solve {=r*n} − {r}t = 0.","explain":"{r}t = {=r*n}, so t = {=r*n} ÷ {r} = {n} minutes. After that the model would give negative volumes.","params":{"r":[10,30],"n":[10,25]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ana saves the same amount each week. After 3 weeks she has £47. After 8 weeks she has £87. Which model fits?","choices":["S = 23 + 8w","S = 47 + 8w","S = 40w"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rate = (87 − 47) ÷ 5. Then go back 3 weeks from £47.","explain":"Rate = 40 ÷ 5 = £8 per week. Start = 47 − 3 × 8 = £23, money she had before she began. 23 + 64 = 87. ✓"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A phone plays video. After {a} h it has {=b-k*a}% battery. After {=a+3} h it has {=b-k*(a+3)}%. How many percentage points does it lose per hour?","answer":"k","hint":"The battery fell by {=3*k} points in 3 hours.","explain":"{=3*k} ÷ 3 = {k} points per hour, so the rate is −{k} in the model.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[90,100],"k":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A model for phone battery is B = 92 − 8t. It predicts B = −4 at t = 12. What does that tell you?","choices":["The model no longer makes sense; the phone is flat at t = 11.5","The phone owes 4% of battery","The rate must be wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can battery go below 0%? When does B reach 0?","explain":"92 − 8t = 0 at t = 11.5. Past that, the model gives impossible values, so it only holds for 0 ≤ t ≤ 11.5."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which of these can be modelled well by a straight line?","choices":["A pool filling from a hose at 15 L per minute","Savings that earn 5% interest each year","The height of a ball that is thrown up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the same change for every equal step.","explain":"The hose adds 15 L every minute, the same each step. Interest grows by more each year, and a ball slows, stops and falls, so neither is linear."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A candle burns steadily. After {x} h it is {=s-r*x} cm tall. After {=x+d} h it is {=s-r*(x+d)} cm tall. How many cm does it lose each hour?","answer":"r","hint":"Change in height ÷ change in time. The time changed by {d} hours.","explain":"It lost {=r*d} cm in {d} hours, so the rate is {=r*d} ÷ {d} = {r} cm per hour (a rate of −{r} in the model).","params":{"s":[30,40],"r":[1,3],"x":[1,3],"d":[2,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A plumber charges a call-out fee plus a fixed amount per hour. A 2-hour job costs £{=c+2*r}. A 4-hour job costs £{=c+4*r}. What is the call-out fee in pounds?","answer":"c","hint":"First find the cost per hour: (£{=c+4*r} − £{=c+2*r}) ÷ 2. Then work back from the 2-hour job.","explain":"Rate = £{=2*r} ÷ 2 = £{r} per hour. Call-out fee = £{=c+2*r} − 2 × £{r} = £{c}.","params":{"c":[20,60],"r":[25,45]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A gym costs £95 for 3 months and £175 for 7 months, including a joining fee. Which model fits?","choices":["C = 35 + 20m","C = 95 + 20m","C = 25m"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rate = (175 − 95) ÷ (7 − 3). Then work back 3 months from £95.","explain":"Rate = 80 ÷ 4 = £20 per month. Start = 95 − 3 × 20 = £35. Check: 35 + 7 × 20 = 175. ✓"},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A spring's length is L = 13 + 3m cm, where m is the mass hung on it in kg. What does the 13 mean?","choices":["The length with no mass on it","How much it stretches per kg","The length with 1 kg on it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put m = 0 into the model.","explain":"When m = 0, L = 13. So 13 cm is the natural length. The 3 is the stretch per kg."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A candle's height is h = 30 − 3t cm after t hours. For which times does the model make sense?","choices":["0 ≤ t ≤ 10","Any time t","0 ≤ t ≤ 30"],"answer":0,"hint":"When does the height reach 0?","explain":"30 − 3t = 0 when t = 10. After that the model gives negative heights, which cannot happen."}],"explain":{"kid":["A candle burns down at a steady speed. After 2 hours it is 18 cm tall. After 5 hours it is 12 cm tall.","From 2 hours to 5 hours is 3 hours. In that time it got 6 cm shorter. So it loses 6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm each hour.","Now go back to when it was lit. That is 2 hours before the first reading, so add 2 × 2 = 4 cm. It started at 22 cm.","So the height is 22 take away 2 for every hour: h = 22 − 2t. At 11 hours the candle is gone, so the rule only works up to 11 hours."],"adult":["A context is linear when equal steps in the input give equal changes in the output: a steady burn, a fixed hourly charge, a spring stretching the same amount per kilogram. The model is y = a + bx, where b is the rate and a is the start value (the output when x = 0).","With two readings (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂), the rate is b = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁), in output units per input unit. Then work back from either reading: a = y₁ − b × x₁. Check the model against the other reading before you trust it.","Read the numbers in context. In C = 35 + 20m for a gym, 35 is the joining fee in pounds and 20 is the cost per month. A falling quantity has a negative rate. A model also has a sensible range: h = 22 − 2t gives negative heights after 11 hours, so it only holds for 0 ≤ t ≤ 11.","Common mistakes: using the first reading given as the start value; dividing one reading by its input (for example 18 ÷ 2), which wrongly assumes the output was 0 at the start; forgetting to divide the change by the number of steps; getting the sign of the rate wrong; and using the model far outside the range of the data."]},"familyTip":"Fill a jug from a tap that runs steadily and note the water level at two times, such as 10 s and 25 s. Work out the rise per second, then work back to see if the jug really started empty. Does your rule predict the level at 20 s?","keyIdea":"When something changes by the same amount for every equal step, a straight line models it: output = start value + rate × input. Get the rate from two readings as change in output ÷ change in input, work back to input 0 for the start value, then say which inputs the model makes sense for.","worked":{"title":"A tank drains steadily. After 3 min it holds 340 L; after 8 min it holds 240 L. Build a model and say when it is empty.","steps":["Change in volume: 240 − 340 = −100 L. Change in time: 8 − 3 = 5 min.","Rate = −100 ÷ 5 = −20 L per minute. It is negative because the tank is emptying.","Start value: work back 3 minutes from the first reading: 340 + 3 × 20 = 400 L. So V = 400 − 20t.","Check: at t = 8, V = 400 − 160 = 240 L. ✓ Empty when 400 − 20t = 0, so t = 20. The model holds for 0 ≤ t ≤ 20."]},"visual":{"type":"formula","caption":"Slide t. The candle model h = 22 − 2t: start at 22 cm, lose 2 cm each hour. Past t = 11 the answer turns negative and the model stops making sense.","opts":{"name":"h","expr":"22 − 2 × t","vars":{"t":[0,12,2]},"what":"candle height","unit":"cm"}},"lab":{"type":"flaglmod","caption":"Flagship lab: get the rate from two candle readings, work back to the start, build the model, find where it stops, then model a gym.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_lzMMW-RVO7":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Is f(x) = x³ + 1 odd, even or neither?","choices":["Neither","Odd","Even"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out f(1) and f(−1). Are they equal, opposite or neither?","explain":"f(1) = 2 and f(−1) = 0. These are neither equal nor opposite, so f is neither. The + 1 spoils the odd symmetry of x³."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Is f(x) = x sin x odd, even or neither?","choices":["Even","Odd","Neither"],"answer":0,"hint":"x is odd and sin x is odd. What is odd × odd?","explain":"f(−x) = (−x)sin(−x) = (−x)(−sin x) = x sin x = f(x), so f is even."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why is cos x an even function?","choices":["cos(−x) = cos x, so its graph is symmetric in the y-axis","cos 0 = 1","Its graph passes through the origin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the cosine graph on each side of the y-axis.","explain":"The angle −x has the same x-coordinate on the unit circle as x, so cos(−x) = cos x. That is the even rule."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"f is odd and defined for every x. What is f(0)?","choices":["0","Any number","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put x = 0 into f(−x) = −f(x).","explain":"f(−0) = −f(0) means f(0) = −f(0), so 2f(0) = 0 and f(0) = 0. The graph must pass through the origin."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ana says f(x) = (x − 1)² is even because its graph is a symmetric U. What is wrong?","choices":["Its mirror line is x = 1, not the y-axis","Nothing: it is even","It is odd, because of the minus sign"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where is the vertex of (x − 1)²?","explain":"Even means symmetric in the y-axis. (x − 1)² is symmetric about x = 1: f(−1) = 4 but f(1) = 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ben checks f(2) = −f(−2) for one function and says it is odd. Is that enough?","choices":["No: the rule must hold for every x","Yes: one pair proves it","Yes, if f(2) is positive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Could another x break the rule?","explain":"One pair can rule a test out but cannot prove it. For example x⁴ − 4x² gives f(2) = 0 = −f(−2), yet it is even, not odd."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which function is odd?","choices":["x⁵ − 2x³ + x","x⁵ + 1","x⁴ + x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Odd needs only odd powers and no constant.","explain":"Every term in x⁵ − 2x³ + x is an odd power, so each flips sign and f(−x) = −f(x). The other two mix odd and even powers."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"f(x) = x⁶ + 2x² − 7. What does the constant −7 do to the symmetry?","choices":["Nothing: −7 = −7x⁰, so f stays even","It makes f neither","It makes f odd"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does −7 change when x becomes −x?","explain":"A constant is the same at x and −x, so it keeps f(−x) = f(x). Moving the graph up or down keeps y-axis symmetry."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"h is odd and h(−{a}) = {b}. What is h({a})?","answer":"-b","hint":"Odd means h(x) = −h(−x). Flip the sign of the output you know.","explain":"h({a}) = −h(−{a}) = −{b}. The input changes sign and so does the output.","params":{"a":[2,7],"b":[3,11]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"f(x) = x³ + {a}. What is f(0)?","answer":"a","hint":"Put x = 0 into the formula. Then ask: could an odd function have this value at 0?","explain":"f(0) = 0³ + {a} = {a}. That is not 0, so f cannot be odd: an odd function defined at 0 must have f(0) = 0.","params":{"a":[2,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"f is an odd function and f({a}) = {b}. What is f(−{a})?","answer":"-b","hint":"Odd means f(−x) = −f(x): the output keeps its size but flips its sign.","explain":"For an odd function the output flips sign, so f(−{a}) = −f({a}) = −{b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[3,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"g is an even function and g(−{a}) = {b}. What is g({a})?","answer":"b","hint":"Even means the outputs at x and −x are equal.","explain":"For an even function g(x) = g(−x), so g({a}) = g(−{a}) = {b}.","params":{"a":[1,8],"b":[2,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which of these functions is even?","choices":["x⁴ − 3x² + 5","x³ + x","x² + x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a function with only even powers. A constant counts as x⁰.","explain":"x⁴, x² and the constant 5 all keep their sign when x becomes −x, so f(−x) = f(x). x³ + x is odd and x² + x is neither."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"What is f(−x) when f(x) = x³ − 4x?","choices":["−x³ + 4x, which is −f(x)","x³ − 4x, which is f(x)","−x³ − 4x"],"answer":0,"hint":"(−x)³ = −x³ and −4(−x) = +4x.","explain":"f(−x) = (−x)³ − 4(−x) = −x³ + 4x = −(x³ − 4x) = −f(x), so f is odd."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A graph is unchanged by a half turn about the origin. What kind of function is it?","choices":["Odd","Even","Neither"],"answer":0,"hint":"A half turn sends (x, y) to (−x, −y).","explain":"If (x, y) on the graph always gives (−x, −y) on the graph, then f(−x) = −f(x): the function is odd."}],"explain":{"kid":["Put −x into a function and see what comes out. If f(−x) = f(x) every time, f is even. The graph is the same on both sides of the y-axis, like a fold. x² is even, since (−3)² = 9 = 3².","If f(−x) = −f(x) every time, f is odd. Each output keeps its size but flips its sign. Turn the graph a half turn about O and it lands on itself. x³ is odd, since (−2)³ = −8.","Many functions are neither. x³ + 1 is not odd: f(1) = 2 but f(−1) = 0. One pair that fails is enough to rule a test out. But one pair that works does not prove a test passes.","For a polynomial, check the powers. Even powers and constants keep their sign. Odd powers flip. So x⁴ − 4x² is even and x³ − 4x is odd. cos x is even and sin x is odd. If f is odd and defined at 0, then f(0) = 0."],"adult":["This A-level topic (ages 16 to 18) asks learners to classify functions as even, with f(−x) = f(x) and symmetry in the y-axis, or odd, with f(−x) = −f(x) and rotational symmetry of order 2 about the origin. The tests must hold for every x in a domain that is symmetric about 0.","The algebraic test is to simplify f(−x) and compare it with f(x) and with −f(x). For polynomials this reduces to the powers: only even powers (constants count as x⁰) gives even, only odd powers gives odd. Products follow sign rules: odd × odd is even, odd × even is odd, so x sin x is even.","Common mistakes: calling x³ + 1 odd because x³ is; thinking a constant breaks evenness; judging from one pair of values, which can only disprove a test; reading (x − 1)² as even because it is a mirror shape about another line; and forgetting that an odd function defined at 0 must pass through the origin.","In the lab, learners drag a point P and watch its partner Q at −x against a mirror ring and a half-turn ring. They turn a copy of x³ and x², sort six graphs, build an odd graph from its right half to hit two targets, and switch polynomial powers on and off. The challenge mixes classifying with using f(−a)."]},"familyTip":"Fold a sheet of squared paper down the middle and draw half a curve on one side. Press it to copy onto the other side: that is an even graph. Then try turning the sheet upside down to make an odd one.","keyIdea":"A function is even when f(−x) = f(x) for every x: its graph is its own mirror image in the y-axis. It is odd when f(−x) = −f(x) for every x: a half turn about the origin leaves its graph unchanged. Most functions are neither. To test one, work out f(−x) and compare it with f(x) and with −f(x).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Even, odd or neither? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Even","Odd","Neither"],"items":[["x⁴ + 1",0],["cos x",0],["x³ − 4x",1],["sin x",1],["x² + x",2],["(x − 1)²",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagoddev","caption":"Flagship lab: drag a point and its partner at −x, turn graphs half a turn, sort and build odd and even graphs, and switch powers on and off.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_3bLrl-cWAS":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"x = 3t, y = t². Which is the Cartesian equation?","choices":["y = x²/9","y = x²/3","y = 9x²"],"answer":0,"hint":"Make t the subject of x = 3t.","explain":"t = x/3, so y = (x/3)² = x²/9."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which parametric equations describe a circle of radius 4 centred at the origin?","choices":["x = 4 cos t, y = 4 sin t","x = 4t, y = 4t","x = cos 4t, y = sin t"],"answer":0,"hint":"A circle of radius r is x = r cos t, y = r sin t.","explain":"x = 4 cos t, y = 4 sin t gives x² + y² = 16, a circle of radius 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"x = t², y = t⁴ for all real t. Which describes the curve?","choices":["The part of y = x² with x ≥ 0","The whole parabola y = x²","The part of y = x² with x ≤ 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can x = t² ever be negative? Think about the sign of x.","explain":"y = (t²)² = x², but x = t² ≥ 0, so only the right half of the parabola is traced."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"x = {a} cos t, y = {a} sin t. What is the radius of the circle?","answer":"a","hint":"x² + y² = r² for x = r cos t, y = r sin t.","explain":"x² + y² = {a}², so the radius is {a}.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"x = cos t, y = sin t for 0 ≤ t ≤ π. What is traced?","choices":["The top half of the unit circle","The whole unit circle","The bottom half of the unit circle"],"answer":0,"hint":"What sign does sin t have for 0 ≤ t ≤ π?","explain":"sin t ≥ 0 for 0 ≤ t ≤ π, so y ≥ 0: the top half of x² + y² = 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"x = t − {a}, y = 3t. Find y at the point where x = {b}.","answer":"3*(b+a)","hint":"Rearrange x = t − {a} to find t first.","explain":"t = x + {a} = {b} + {a} = {=b+a}, so y = 3 × {=b+a} = {=3*(b+a)}.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"x = 5 cos t, y = 2 sin t. How wide is the curve, from left to right?","choices":["10","5","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"cos t runs from −1 to 1, so x runs from −5 to 5.","explain":"cos t runs from −1 to 1, so x runs from −5 to 5: a width of 10. The height is 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"x = t − 2, y = t². Which is the Cartesian equation?","choices":["y = (x + 2)²","y = (x − 2)²","y = x² + 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Undo the − 2 first: from x = t − 2 you get t = x + 2.","explain":"t = x + 2, so y = (x + 2)². Writing (x − 2)² undoes the shift the wrong way."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"x = {a}t, y = t². At t = {b}, what is the y-coordinate of the point?","answer":"b*b","hint":"Substitute t = {b} into y = t², so square {b}.","explain":"y = t² = {b}² = {=b*b}. The x-coordinate is {a} × {b} = {=a*b}, so the point is ({=a*b}, {=b*b}).","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"x = 2t, y = t². Which is the Cartesian equation?","choices":["y = x²/4","y = x²/2","y = 2x²"],"answer":0,"hint":"t = x/2. Square all of it.","explain":"t = x/2, so y = (x/2)² = x²/4. Writing x²/2 forgets to square the 2."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"x = {r} cos t, y = {r} sin t. The curve is x² + y² = k. What is k?","answer":"r*r","hint":"x² + y² = r²cos²t + r²sin²t. Factor out r².","explain":"x² + y² = {r}²(cos²t + sin²t) = {r}² = {=r*r}. It is a circle of radius {r}, so k = {=r*r}.","params":{"r":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"x = 3 cos t, y = 2 sin t. Which is the Cartesian equation?","choices":["(x/3)² + (y/2)² = 1","x/3 + y/2 = 1","x² + y² = 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Isolate cos t and sin t, then use cos²t + sin²t = 1.","explain":"cos t = x/3 and sin t = y/2, so (x/3)² + (y/2)² = 1: an ellipse. Adding x/3 + y/2 gives a line, because cos t + sin t is not 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"x = t + {a}, y = 2t. Find y when x = {b}.","answer":"2*(b-a)","hint":"First find t from x = t + {a}.","explain":"t = x − {a} = {b} − {a} = {=b-a}, so y = 2 × {=b-a} = {=2*(b-a)}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[5,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["In a parametric curve, x and y each depend on a third variable t, called the parameter. Take x = 2t and y = t². Pick t = −1. Then x = −2 and y = 1, so the point is (−2, 1). A table of t values gives a set of points. Join them in order of t to get the curve.","To get a Cartesian equation, remove t. From x = 2t, t = x/2. Put that into y = t² to get y = (x/2)² = x²/4. The common slip is y = x²/2. The 2 must be squared too.","When t is inside sin and cos, use cos²t + sin²t = 1. For x = 3 cos t and y = 2 sin t, cos t = x/3 and sin t = y/2. So (x/3)² + (y/2)² = 1, an ellipse 6 wide and 4 tall. For x = r cos t and y = r sin t you get x² + y² = r², a circle of radius r.","Always check the range. x = t² is never negative. So x = t², y = t⁴ traces only the right half of y = x². And x = cos t, y = sin t for 0 ≤ t ≤ π gives only the top half of the unit circle."],"adult":["This A-level topic (ages 17 to 18) describes curves with parametric equations x = f(t), y = g(t). Students plot curves from a table of parameter values, convert to Cartesian form by eliminating the parameter, and write the parametric form of a circle, x = r cos t, y = r sin t. It builds on functions, trigonometric identities and graph sketching, and leads to parametric differentiation and integration.","There are two main ways to eliminate the parameter. If one equation is easy to rearrange, make t the subject and substitute into the other: x = 2t, y = t² gives y = x²/4. If t appears inside sine and cosine, isolate cos t and sin t and use cos²t + sin²t = 1: x = a cos t, y = b sin t gives x²/a² + y²/b² = 1. The circle is the special case a = b = r.","Common mistakes: squaring only part of an expression, so (x/2)² becomes x²/2; dividing when you should multiply or the reverse when making t the subject; writing x + y = r or x² + y² = r for a circle of radius r; adding cos t + sin t = 1 instead of squaring; and forgetting the domain, so a curve such as x = t², y = t⁴ is described as the whole parabola y = x² when x can never be negative. Be careful to state any restriction on x or y with the Cartesian equation.","The lab has five parts. You step t along a track to plot x = 2t, y = t². You eliminate t card by card and see y = x²/4 run through the points. You set r and sweep t to trace a circle while x² + y² stays fixed. You build the ellipse equation from the identity. Last, you sweep two restricted curves and decide which part of the Cartesian graph they really cover."]},"familyTip":"Ask your teenager to explain a moving point as a story: at time t the point is at (2t, t²), so where is it at t = 0, 1 and 2, and which way is it going? Then ask what equation links x and y once time is taken away.","keyIdea":"A parametric curve gives x and y separately in terms of a parameter t: x = f(t), y = g(t). Each value of t gives one point, so you can plot the curve from a table of t values. To find the Cartesian equation, eliminate t: make t the subject of one equation and substitute, or use an identity such as cos²t + sin²t = 1. x = r cos t, y = r sin t is a circle of radius r. Check the range: the parametric curve may cover only part of the Cartesian graph.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each pair of parametric equations a circle centred at the origin? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Circle","Not a circle"],"items":[["x = 2 cos t, y = 2 sin t",0],["x = 3 cos t, y = 2 sin t",1],["x = 5 sin t, y = 5 cos t",0],["x = t, y = t²",1],["x = cos t, y = sin t",0],["x = 2t, y = 2t",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagparam","caption":"Flagship lab: plot from t, eliminate t card by card, sweep a circle, use cos²t + sin²t = 1, and find which part a restricted curve covers.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_pr_8DYFhDi":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is cosh 0?","choices":["1","0","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"e⁰ = 1. Put x = 0 into (eˣ + e⁻ˣ)/2.","explain":"cosh 0 = (1 + 1)/2 = 1. It is sinh 0 that equals 0, and 2 comes from forgetting the ÷2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which expression equals tanh x?","choices":["(eˣ − e⁻ˣ)/(eˣ + e⁻ˣ)","(eˣ + e⁻ˣ)/(eˣ − e⁻ˣ)","(eˣ − e⁻ˣ)/2"],"answer":0,"hint":"tanh = sinh/cosh. What happens to the two ÷2s?","explain":"tanh x = [(eˣ − e⁻ˣ)/2] ÷ [(eˣ + e⁻ˣ)/2]; the ½s cancel. The flipped fraction is coth x, and (eˣ − e⁻ˣ)/2 is sinh x."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"For some x, cosh x + sinh x = {a}. What is eˣ?","answer":"a","tolerance":0.001,"hint":"Add the two definitions. What happens to the e⁻ˣ terms?","explain":"cosh x + sinh x = (eˣ + e⁻ˣ)/2 + (eˣ − e⁻ˣ)/2 = eˣ, so eˣ = {a}.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"sinh x = {s}. Use cosh²x − sinh²x = 1 to find cosh²x.","answer":"s*s+1","tolerance":0.001,"hint":"Rearrange: cosh²x = 1 + sinh²x.","explain":"cosh²x = 1 + sinh²x = 1 + {s}² = {=s*s+1}.","params":{"s":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which statement is true for every x?","choices":["sinh(−x) = −sinh x","sinh(−x) = sinh x","cosh(−x) = −cosh x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Replacing x with −x swaps eˣ and e⁻ˣ. Which definition changes sign?","explain":"sinh is odd: swapping eˣ and e⁻ˣ flips the sign of the difference. cosh is even, since the sum does not change."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Using a calculator, what is sinh 1 to 4 significant figures?","choices":["1.175","2.350","1.543"],"answer":0,"hint":"sinh 1 = (e − e⁻¹)/2, with e ≈ 2.71828.","explain":"(2.71828 − 0.36788)/2 = 1.175. 2.350 forgets the ÷2, and 1.543 is cosh 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is the smallest value cosh x can take?","choices":["1, at x = 0","0, at x = 0","It has no smallest value"],"answer":0,"hint":"cosh is the midpoint of eˣ and e⁻ˣ, and both are positive.","explain":"cosh x ≥ 1, with minimum cosh 0 = 1. The graph is a U-shape, the catenary, with its lowest point at (0, 1)."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is the definition of cosh x?","choices":["(eˣ + e⁻ˣ)/2","(eˣ − e⁻ˣ)/2","eˣ + e⁻ˣ"],"answer":0,"hint":"cosh is the midpoint of eˣ and e⁻ˣ: add, then halve.","explain":"cosh x = (eˣ + e⁻ˣ)/2. The minus version is sinh x, and eˣ + e⁻ˣ without the ÷2 is 2cosh x."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"At some value of x, eˣ + e⁻ˣ = {s}. What is cosh x?","answer":"s/2","tolerance":0.001,"hint":"cosh x is the sum eˣ + e⁻ˣ, halved.","explain":"cosh x = (eˣ + e⁻ˣ)/2 = {s}/2 = {=s/2}.","params":{"s":[3,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"At some value of x, eˣ − e⁻ˣ = {d}. What is sinh(−x)?","answer":"-d/2","tolerance":0.001,"hint":"First find sinh x. Then use the fact that sinh is odd.","explain":"sinh x = {d}/2, and sinh(−x) = −sinh x, so sinh(−x) = {=-d/2}.","params":{"d":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which values can tanh x take?","choices":["Only values between −1 and 1","Any real value","Only values of 1 or more"],"answer":0,"hint":"For large x, sinh x and cosh x are both about eˣ/2.","explain":"−1 < tanh x < 1. The ratio sinh/cosh tends to 1 as x → ∞ and to −1 as x → −∞, without reaching either. Values of 1 or more belong to cosh."}],"explain":{"kid":["Take two curves, y = eˣ and y = e⁻ˣ. They cross at (0, 1). Add them and halve: that is cosh x, the midpoint of the two curves. So cosh x = (eˣ + e⁻ˣ)/2. At x = 0 both are 1, so cosh 0 = 1.","Now take the gap and halve it: sinh x = (eˣ − e⁻ˣ)/2. So sinh 0 = 0. Swap x for −x and eˣ swaps with e⁻ˣ. The sum stays the same, so cosh(−x) = cosh x. The gap flips sign, so sinh(−x) = −sinh x.","Divide them to get tanh x = sinh x / cosh x. The ½s cancel, so tanh x = (eˣ − e⁻ˣ)/(eˣ + e⁻ˣ). For big x, e⁻ˣ is tiny and both parts are about eˣ/2. So tanh x gets close to 1 but never reaches it.","Why 'hyperbolic'? Square them and subtract: cosh²t − sinh²t = 1 for every t. So the point (cosh t, sinh t) is on the hyperbola x² − y² = 1. In the same way, (cos t, sin t) is on the circle x² + y² = 1."],"adult":["Hyperbolic functions are in A level Further Mathematics (Core Pure) and IB Analysis and Approaches HL extension work. Students must state the exponential definitions of sinh, cosh and tanh, and evaluate them with a calculator's hyp key. Later work builds on this: identities, graphs, inverse functions such as arsinh, and calculus.","Common mistakes: losing the ÷2, which doubles every answer, and swapping the signs, so cosh gets the minus. Students often write cosh 0 = 0 by analogy with sin 0. For tanh, many write (eˣ − e⁻ˣ)/2 over 2 again, or flip the fraction, which gives coth.","Useful checks follow from the definitions. cosh x + sinh x = eˣ and cosh x − sinh x = e⁻ˣ; multiplying them gives cosh²x − sinh²x = 1, the analogue of cos²x + sin²x = 1 with a sign change. cosh has minimum 1 at x = 0, and tanh has horizontal asymptotes y = ±1.","In the lab the learner drags x to see cosh as the midpoint of eˣ and e⁻ˣ, compares sinh(x) with sinh(−x), pushes tanh towards its asymptotes, traces (cosh t, sinh t) along the hyperbola, and builds sinh 1, cosh 2 and tanh 0.5 from numerator and denominator tiles."]},"familyTip":"Hang a light chain between two hooks: its curve is a cosh curve, called a catenary. Ask your learner to say why its lowest point is where x = 0, and what cosh 0 is.","keyIdea":"The hyperbolic functions are built from eˣ and e⁻ˣ: cosh x = (eˣ + e⁻ˣ)/2, sinh x = (eˣ − e⁻ˣ)/2 and tanh x = sinh x / cosh x = (eˣ − e⁻ˣ)/(eˣ + e⁻ˣ). So cosh 0 = 1 and sinh 0 = 0; cosh is even with cosh x ≥ 1, sinh is odd, and −1 < tanh x < 1. The point (cosh t, sinh t) lies on the hyperbola x² − y² = 1, because cosh²t − sinh²t = 1. On a calculator, sinh 1 ≈ 1.1752, cosh 2 ≈ 3.7622 and tanh 0.5 ≈ 0.4621.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"True or false for hyperbolic functions? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✓ True","✗ False"],"items":[["cosh 0 = 1",0],["sinh(−x) = −sinh x",0],["cosh²x − sinh²x = 1",0],["−1 < tanh x < 1",0],["cosh x = (eˣ − e⁻ˣ)/2",1],["sinh 0 = 1",1],["tanh x = (eˣ − e⁻ˣ)/2",1],["cosh x can be 0",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghyper","caption":"Flagship lab: cosh as the midpoint of eˣ and e⁻ˣ, sinh as half the gap, tanh's asymptotes, the hyperbola x² − y² = 1, and evaluating values.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_v8xr0RK1hL":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"f(x) = {a}x − {b}. Find f({c}).","answer":"a*c-b","hint":"Put {c} in place of x.","explain":"f({c}) = {a} × {c} − {b} = {=a*c-b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9],"c":[2,9]},"where":"a*c>b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"h(x) = x² + {b}. Find h(−{c}).","answer":"c*c+b","hint":"A negative times a negative is positive.","explain":"(−{c})² = {=c*c}, so h(−{c}) = {=c*c} + {b} = {=c*c+b}.","params":{"b":[1,9],"c":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"f(x) = 3x. Sam says f(2) means 'f × 2'. What is wrong?","choices":["f(2) means put 2 into the rule, so f(2) = 6","Nothing: f(2) = 2f","f(2) means 3 + 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"The brackets show the input.","explain":"f(2) is 'f of 2': 3 × 2 = 6."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"f(x) = {a}x + {b}. For which x is f(x) = {=a*k+b}?","answer":"k","hint":"Set {a}x + {b} = {=a*k+b} and solve.","explain":"{a}x + {b} = {=a*k+b}, so {a}x = {=a*k}, so x = {k}.","params":{"a":[2,7],"b":[1,9],"k":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A taxi ride costs C(m) = 3 + 2m pounds, where m is the number of miles. What does C(5) = 13 mean?","choices":["A 5-mile ride costs £13","A £5 ride goes 13 miles","5 rides cost £13"],"answer":0,"hint":"The input is miles; the output is pounds.","explain":"C(5) = 13: an input of 5 miles gives an output of £13."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"f(x) = x² − 1. Find f(−2).","answer":3,"hint":"Square −2 first.","explain":"(−2)² = 4, and 4 − 1 = 3."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"f(x) = 2x + 1. What is f(3) + f(1)?","choices":["10","9","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out f(3) and f(1) separately.","explain":"f(3) = 7 and f(1) = 3, so the sum is 10. It is not f(4) = 9: you can't just add the inputs."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"f(x) = 2x + 3. Find f(4).","answer":11,"hint":"Put 4 in place of x.","explain":"f(4) = 2 × 4 + 3 = 11."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"f(x) = {a}x + {b}. Find f({c}).","answer":"a*c+b","hint":"Put {c} in place of x.","explain":"f({c}) = {a} × {c} + {b} = {=a*c+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9],"c":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What does f(5) mean?","choices":["The output when the input is 5","f multiplied by 5","The input when the output is 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read it as 'f of 5'.","explain":"f(5) is the output of the rule when 5 goes in."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"g(x) = x² − {b}. Find g({c}).","answer":"c*c-b","hint":"Square {c} first, then subtract.","explain":"g({c}) = {c}² − {b} = {=c*c} − {b} = {=c*c-b}.","params":{"b":[1,9],"c":[2,9]},"where":"c*c>b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"f(x) = {a}x + {b} and f(k) = {=a*k+b}. Find k.","answer":"k","hint":"Solve {a}k + {b} = {=a*k+b}.","explain":"{a}k + {b} = {=a*k+b}, so {a}k = {=a*k}, so k = {k}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,9],"k":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["f(x) = 2x + 3 is a rule. The letter f is its name and x is the input. To find f(4), put 4 in place of x: 2 × 4 + 3 = 11. So f(4) = 11.","f(x) does not mean f times x. Read it as 'f of x': the output when x goes in."],"adult":["Function notation names a rule and its input. If f(x) = x² − 1, then f(3) = 9 − 1 = 8 and f(−2) = 4 − 1 = 3. The brackets after f show the input. They do not mean multiply.","You can also work backwards. If f(x) = 2x + 3 and f(a) = 11, then 2a + 3 = 11, so a = 4. In context, f(3) = 10 might mean 'after 3 hours, 10 litres are left'."]},"familyTip":"Make a function machine: write a rule like 'double it and add 3', then test each other with inputs.","keyIdea":"f(x) is the output of the rule f when the input is x.","visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"Drag the input x along the graph of f(x) = 2x + 3 and read the output.","opts":{"mode":"eval","f":"2*x+3","a":2,"xr":[-1,5],"yr":[-1,14],"step":0.5}}},"mt_lRy8j6tZXT":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A cyclist rides {=v*t} km in {t} hours. What is her average speed in km/h?","answer":"v","hint":"Distance ÷ time.","explain":"{=v*t} ÷ {t} = {v} km/h.","params":{"v":[8,25],"t":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"f(x) = {a}x + {b}. Find the average rate of change from x = 1 to x = 5.","answer":"a","hint":"(f(5) − f(1)) ÷ 4.","explain":"f(5) − f(1) = {=a*4}, and {=a*4} ÷ 4 = {a}. For a straight line it is always the gradient.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"f(x) = x² + {c}. Find the average rate of change from x = {a} to x = {b}.","answer":"a+b","hint":"The + {c} cancels when you subtract.","explain":"f({b}) − f({a}) = {=b*b-a*a} and {b} − {a} = {=b-a}, so {=b*b-a*a} ÷ {=b-a} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[0,5],"b":[1,7],"c":[1,9]},"where":"b>a"},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"On a graph, the average rate of change between two points is…","choices":["the gradient of the straight line joining them","the height of the higher point","the area under the graph"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think rise over run.","explain":"It is the gradient of the line (chord) joining the two points."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A phone cost £600 new and £360 after 3 years. What is the average rate of change of its value?","choices":["−£80 per year","£80 per year","−£240 per year"],"answer":0,"hint":"(360 − 600) ÷ 3.","explain":"(360 − 600) ÷ 3 = −240 ÷ 3 = −80. It lost £80 per year on average."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A runner's average speed over a race was 4 m/s. Which must be true?","choices":["Total distance ÷ total time was 4 m/s","She ran at exactly 4 m/s all the way","Her top speed was 4 m/s"],"answer":0,"hint":"An average can hide changes.","explain":"She may have sped up and slowed down; only the overall distance ÷ time is 4 m/s."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"f(x) = x². Find the average rate of change from x = 2 to x = 4.","answer":6,"hint":"(f(4) − f(2)) ÷ 2.","explain":"(16 − 4) ÷ (4 − 2) = 12 ÷ 2 = 6."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A car travels 120 km in 2 hours. What is its average speed in km per hour?","answer":60,"hint":"Distance ÷ time.","explain":"120 ÷ 2 = 60 km per hour."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A plant is {h} cm tall on day 0 and {=h+r*d} cm tall on day {d}. What is its average growth in cm per day?","answer":"r","hint":"Change in height ÷ change in time.","explain":"It grows {=r*d} cm in {d} days: {=r*d} ÷ {d} = {r} cm per day.","params":{"h":[2,9],"r":[1,5],"d":[2,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"f(x) = x². Find the average rate of change from x = 1 to x = 3.","answer":4,"hint":"(f(3) − f(1)) ÷ (3 − 1).","explain":"f(3) − f(1) = 9 − 1 = 8, and 8 ÷ 2 = 4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"f(x) = x². Find the average rate of change from x = {a} to x = {b}.","answer":"a+b","hint":"(f({b}) − f({a})) ÷ ({b} − {a}).","explain":"f({b}) − f({a}) = {=b*b} − {=a*a} = {=b*b-a*a}. Divide by {=b-a} to get {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[0,6],"b":[1,8]},"where":"b>a"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A tank holds 50 litres at 2 pm and 20 litres at 5 pm. What is the average rate of change?","choices":["−10 litres per hour","10 litres per hour","−30 litres per hour"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change in litres ÷ change in hours. Did it go up or down?","explain":"(20 − 50) ÷ 3 = −10 litres per hour. Negative means it emptied."}],"explain":{"kid":["A car travels 120 km in 2 hours. Its average speed is 120 ÷ 2 = 60 km per hour. That is an average rate of change.","On a graph, join the two points with a straight line. Its gradient is the average rate of change. It doesn't matter how the graph wiggles in between."],"adult":["For f between x = a and x = b, the average rate of change is (f(b) − f(a)) ÷ (b − a). For f(x) = x² from x = 1 to x = 3, that is (9 − 1) ÷ 2 = 4.","The units are output units per input unit, such as km per hour or pounds per year. A negative answer means the amount fell on average."]},"familyTip":"On a journey, note the time and distance twice. Work out your average speed between them.","keyIdea":"Average rate of change = change in output ÷ change in input. On a graph it is the gradient of the straight line joining two points.","visual":{"type":"rate","caption":"Drag the two points on y = x². The red line's gradient is the average rate of change.","opts":{"f":"x*x","a":1,"b":3,"xr":[-1,5],"yr":[-2,20]}},"lab":{"type":"graphlab","caption":"Make the curve pass through both targets, then work out the change in y over the change in x.","opts":{"f":"a*x^2+b*x+c","a":1,"b":0,"c":0,"xr":[-1,5],"yr":[-2,20],"targets":[[1,1],[3,9]]}}},"mt_qCaCEPFbTc":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"On a velocity–time graph, the gradient is…","choices":["acceleration","speed","distance"],"answer":0,"hint":"(m/s) ÷ s.","explain":"(m/s) ÷ s = m/s²: acceleration."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"On an energy–time graph, the gradient is…","choices":["power","force","energy"],"answer":0,"hint":"J ÷ s.","explain":"J ÷ s = W: power."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Velocity rises from {u} m/s to {v} m/s in {t} s. What is the gradient, in m/s²?","answer":"(v-u)/t","hint":"Change in velocity ÷ time.","explain":"({v} − {u}) ÷ {t} = {=(v-u)/t} m/s².","params":{"u":[0,9],"v":[10,30],"t":[2,5]},"where":"((v-u)*100)%t==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A constant {v} m/s for {t} s. What is the area under the velocity–time graph, in m?","answer":"v*t","hint":"Rectangle.","explain":"{v} × {t} = {=v*t} m.","params":{"v":[2,15],"t":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What are the units of the gradient of a distance–time graph?","choices":["m/s","m/s²","s/m"],"answer":0,"hint":"y units ÷ x units.","explain":"m ÷ s = m/s."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Does the area under a distance–time graph mean anything useful?","choices":["Not usually: m × s is not a useful quantity","Yes: it is the speed","Yes: it is the acceleration"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply the units.","explain":"m × s has no everyday physical meaning."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A curved distance–time graph gets steeper. The object is…","choices":["speeding up","slowing down","stopped"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gradient = speed.","explain":"The gradient (speed) is increasing."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"On a distance–time graph, the gradient is…","choices":["speed","acceleration","distance"],"answer":0,"hint":"m ÷ s.","explain":"m ÷ s = m/s: speed."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A distance–time graph rises {d} m in {t} s. What is the speed in m/s?","answer":"d/t","hint":"Gradient = rise ÷ run.","explain":"{d} ÷ {t} = {=d/t} m/s.","params":{"d":[10,90],"t":[2,5]},"where":"(d*100)%t==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A power–time graph is flat at {p} W for {t} s. How much energy is that, in J?","answer":"p*t","hint":"Area = power × time.","explain":"{p} × {t} = {=p*t} J.","params":{"p":[10,90],"t":[2,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"On a velocity–time graph, the area under the line is…","choices":["displacement","acceleration","speed"],"answer":0,"hint":"(m/s) × s.","explain":"(m/s) × s = m: displacement."}],"explain":{"kid":["A graph's steepness shows a rate. On a distance–time graph, steep means fast.","The area under some graphs shows a total. On a speed–time graph, the area is the distance. Always check the units on the axes to see what the gradient and the area mean."],"adult":["To read a gradient, divide the y units by the x units. On a distance–time graph, m ÷ s gives speed. On a velocity–time graph you get acceleration. On an energy–time graph, J ÷ s is power in watts.","To read an area, multiply the units. Velocity × time gives distance, and power × time gives energy. If the units multiply to nothing useful, the area has no real meaning."]},"familyTip":"Pick any graph in a news story. Ask: what would its gradient mean? Does its area mean anything?","keyIdea":"The gradient of a graph is a rate (speed, acceleration, power); the area under a graph is an accumulated amount (distance, energy).","visual":{"type":"rate","caption":"Distance s = t²/2. Drag the two points: the line's gradient is the average speed between them.","opts":{"f":"x*x/2","a":1,"b":3,"xr":[-0.5,5],"yr":[-1,12],"ux":"s","uy":"m"}}},"mt_x68PoRveI0":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which graph fails the vertical line test?","choices":["A circle","A straight line y = 2x","y = x²"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a vertical line hit it twice?","explain":"A vertical line through a circle crosses it twice."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Person → their birthday. Is it a function?","choices":["Yes, each person has one birthday","No, people share birthdays","No, it is not numbers"],"answer":0,"hint":"One output per input.","explain":"Each person has exactly one birthday, so yes. Sharing is allowed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Birthday → person. Is it a function?","choices":["No, one date can match many people","Yes","Only in a small class"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can one input give two outputs?","explain":"One date can belong to several people."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"f(x) = {a}x − {b}. What is f({x})?","answer":"a*x-b","hint":"Substitute.","explain":"{a} × {x} − {b} = {=a*x-b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9],"x":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"g(x) = (x + {a})². What is g({x})?","answer":"(x+a)**2","hint":"Add, then square.","explain":"({x} + {a})² = {=(x+a)**2}.","params":{"a":[1,5],"x":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The pairs (1, 2), (2, 4), (1, 3). Is this a function?","choices":["No","Yes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at input 1.","explain":"Input 1 gives both 2 and 3."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A vertical line x = 4 on a graph is…","choices":["not a function","a function","the function y = 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Input 4 has every output.","explain":"x = 4 gives infinitely many outputs for one input."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A table has input 3 with outputs 5 and 7. Is it a function?","choices":["No, 3 has two outputs","Yes","Only if 5 < 7"],"answer":0,"hint":"One input, one output.","explain":"The input 3 has two outputs, so it is not a function."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Inputs 1 and 2 both give 4. Is that allowed in a function?","choices":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"hint":"It's inputs that must not repeat.","explain":"Different inputs can share an output."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"The rule is 'multiply by {a} then add {b}'. What is the output for input {x}?","answer":"a*x+b","hint":"Do the rule.","explain":"{a} × {x} + {b} = {=a*x+b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,9],"x":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"f(x) = x² − {b}. What is f({x})?","answer":"x*x-b","hint":"Square, then subtract.","explain":"{x}² − {b} = {=x*x-b}.","params":{"b":[1,9],"x":[2,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A function is like a machine. You put a number in and one number comes out.","If the same input could give two different outputs, it is not a function. Two inputs giving the same output is fine."],"adult":["A function assigns exactly one output to each input. Tables, mapping diagrams, graphs and rules can all show functions.","In a table, check that no input appears with two outputs. On a graph, use the vertical line test: if any vertical line hits the graph twice, it is not a function. A circle fails; y = x² passes."]},"familyTip":"Play 'function machine': one person secretly picks a rule, the others give inputs and guess the rule.","keyIdea":"A function gives exactly one output for each input.","visual":{"type":"vlt","caption":"Pick a graph and drag the red vertical line. If it ever hits the graph twice, the graph is not a function.","opts":{"graphs":["line","parab","circle","side","cubic"],"x":1}},"lab":{"type":"graphlab","caption":"Type any rule for y. Each x gives one y, so it is a function. Pass through the targets.","opts":{"f":"a*x+b","a":1,"b":0,"xr":[-5,5],"yr":[-5,9],"targets":[[1,3],[3,7]]}}},"mt_ZBFDXdwUNC":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"y = −(x − 3)² + 9 increases when…","choices":["x < 3","x > 3","always"],"answer":0,"hint":"It's a hill with its top at 3.","explain":"It rises up to x = 3, then falls."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"y = −(x − {h})² + {k}. What is the maximum value?","answer":"k","hint":"The squared part is 0 at best.","explain":"The maximum is {k}.","params":{"h":[1,6],"k":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"y = x² − {=r*r}. Give the positive x-intercept.","answer":"r","hint":"Set y = 0.","explain":"x² = {=r*r}, so x = {r}.","params":{"r":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A graph of temperature over a day peaks at 3 pm. That is…","choices":["the maximum","an intercept","the end behaviour"],"answer":0,"hint":"Highest point.","explain":"The highest point is the maximum."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"y = x² is symmetric about…","choices":["the y-axis","the x-axis","the line y = x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mirror left and right.","explain":"f(−x) = f(x): symmetric about the y-axis."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"As x gets very large, y = −x² …","choices":["goes down without end","goes up","levels off at 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"End behaviour.","explain":"−x² becomes more and more negative."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A tangent slopes down. The graph there is…","choices":["decreasing","increasing","at a maximum"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gradient sign.","explain":"A negative gradient means decreasing."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where is a graph's y-intercept?","choices":["Where x = 0","Where y = 0","At the top"],"answer":0,"hint":"It's on the y-axis.","explain":"On the y-axis, x = 0."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"y = (x − {h})² − 4. At what x is the minimum?","answer":"h","hint":"The square is smallest at 0.","explain":"x − {h} = 0 gives x = {h}.","params":{"h":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"y = {a}x + {b}. What is the y-intercept?","answer":"b","hint":"Put x = 0.","explain":"y = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"At a maximum, the tangent is…","choices":["flat","steep upwards","vertical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Top of a hill.","explain":"Its gradient is 0, so it is flat."}],"explain":{"kid":["A graph tells a story. Going up means getting bigger. Going down means getting smaller.","The top of a hill is a maximum. The bottom of a valley is a minimum. Where the graph crosses an axis is an intercept."],"adult":["Increasing means y rises as x moves right, so the tangent slopes up. Decreasing means it slopes down. At a maximum or minimum, the tangent is flat.","The y-intercept is where x = 0. The x-intercepts are the zeros. Symmetry and end behaviour describe the overall shape, such as a parabola that rises at both ends."]},"familyTip":"Look at a weather app's temperature graph. When was it warmest? When was it rising?","keyIdea":"Read a graph's story from its features: intercepts, where it rises or falls, its highest and lowest points, symmetry and end behaviour.","visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"A hill-shaped graph. Drag the point: the tangent slopes up while increasing, is flat at the maximum, and slopes down after.","opts":{"mode":"tangent","f":"-(x-3)*(x-3)+9","a":1,"xr":[-1,7],"yr":[-4,11],"step":0.5,"label":"f(x)"}},"lab":{"type":"graphlab","caption":"Edit the quadratic. Move it so its roots sit on the red targets.","opts":{"f":"a*x^2+b*x+c","a":1,"b":0,"c":0,"xr":[-5,5],"yr":[-6,10],"targets":[[1,0],[3,0]]}}},"mt_Sa5JjOvfhX":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Running to the bus stop, then waiting, looks like…","choices":["steep up, then flat","flat, then steep","a straight line down"],"answer":0,"hint":"Distance from home.","explain":"Distance rises fast, then stays the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A line sloping back down to zero distance from home means…","choices":["going back home","slowing down","waiting"],"answer":0,"hint":"Distance shrinks.","explain":"Getting closer to home."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Filling a bath, the water depth graph rises then is flat. The flat part means…","choices":["the tap is off","the bath is draining","the tap is fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"No change.","explain":"Depth stays the same, so the tap is off."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A plant's height rises fast, then levels off. Describe it.","choices":["Increasing, then levelling off","Decreasing","Constant"],"answer":0,"hint":"Growth slows.","explain":"It grows quickly at first, then hardly at all."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Walk {a} min, wait {b} min, walk {c} min. How many minutes long is the graph?","answer":"a+b+c","hint":"Add all the times.","explain":"{a} + {b} + {c} = {=a+b+c} min.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,5],"c":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Walk {a} km at a steady pace in {t} min. What is the gradient, in km per min?","answer":"a/t","hint":"Rise ÷ run.","explain":"{a} ÷ {t} = {=a/t} km/min.","params":{"a":[1,4],"t":[10,20]},"where":"(a*100)%t==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which story matches 'rises steadily, flat, then rises more gently'?","choices":["Walk, rest, walk more slowly","Run, rest, run faster","Walk home and stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gentler means slower.","explain":"Second walking part is less steep, so slower."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A flat line on a distance–time graph means…","choices":["not moving","moving fast","coming back"],"answer":0,"hint":"Distance doesn't change.","explain":"No change in distance: standing still."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Walk for {a} min, then wait for {b} min. For how many minutes is the graph flat?","answer":"b","hint":"Flat means waiting.","explain":"The flat part lasts {b} min.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Walk {a} km in {t} min, then rest. How far from home is the flat part, in km?","answer":"a","hint":"Flat means no change.","explain":"It stays at {a} km.","params":{"a":[1,5],"t":[10,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A hot drink cooling quickly, then more slowly. The temperature graph…","choices":["falls steeply, then levels off","is a straight line down","rises"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cooling slows down.","explain":"Steep fall, then levelling off."}],"explain":{"kid":["You can draw a graph from a story, even with no numbers. 'I ran to the bus stop' is a steep line going up.","'I waited' is a flat line. 'The bus took me home fast' is a steep line going back down."],"adult":["Match each part of a story to a shape. Steady change is a straight line. Faster change is steeper. No change is flat. Change that slows down is a curve that levels off.","When you describe a graph, use words like increasing, decreasing, constant, steeper and levelling off. Always say what the axes stand for."]},"familyTip":"Tell a short story about a trip, then each person sketches it. Compare the sketches.","keyIdea":"A graph without numbers can still tell a story: rising, falling, flat, steeper or levelling off.","visual":{"type":"journey","caption":"Run to the bus stop, wait, then ride the bus. Play the story and watch each part of the graph.","opts":{"pts":[[0,0],[5,0.6],[12,0.6],[20,4]],"tu":"min","du":"km","step":1}}}});
